  "justification": "**Operational Excellence**: The proposed architecture introduces ElastiCache for Redis/Memcached, enabling centralized session management across multiple instances and reducing database load through query result caching. This improves observability by decoupling session state from compute instances, allowing instances to be replaced without user impact. Auto Scaling policies can be refined based on CloudWatch metrics from the cache layer and CPU utilization. The db.t3.medium RDS instance provides headroom for monitoring workloads and performance insights without impacting application queries. Automated backups with 7-day retention and point-in-time recovery enable operational resilience and rollback capabilities.\n\n**Security**: The architecture maintains defense in depth with EC2 instances in private subnets accessing the internet only through the NAT Gateway. The Multi-AZ RDS deployment ensures encrypted connections between primary and standby instances. ElastiCache is deployed in private subnets with security groups restricting access to the web tier only. S3 bucket policies enforce encryption at rest and in transit for photo uploads. CloudFront provides DDoS protection via AWS Shield Standard and can be configured with AWS WAF rules. Database credentials are managed through AWS Secrets Manager integration with RDS, and point-in-time recovery enables restoration to any second within the 7-day retention window if a security incident corrupts data. The Application Load Balancer terminates SSL/TLS connections and integrates with AWS Certificate Manager for automated certificate rotation.\n\n**Reliability**: The t3.medium instance type provides 160 requests/second capacity per instance at 70% CPU. With 2 instances minimum, baseline capacity is 320 req/s, exceeding the 150 req/s average requirement with 113% headroom. At peak (750 req/s to origin), 6 instances provide 960 req/s capacity, meeting demand with 28% headroom for burst tolerance. Multi-AZ RDS with automated backups every 5 minutes and 7-day retention satisfies the RPO of 5 minutes. Multi-AZ provides automatic failover typically completing within 1 minute, well under the 1-hour RTO. ElastiCache deployed across 2 AZs ensures session persistence during instance failures. The architecture distributes instances across 2 Availability Zones, meeting requirement N2 for high availability. Auto Scaling responds to CloudWatch CPU alarms, launching additional instances within minutes to handle traffic spikes.\n\n**Performance Efficiency**: Upgrading from t3.micro (40 req/s) to t3.medium (160 req/s) provides 4x capacity per instance, reducing the instance count needed and improving cost efficiency. ElastiCache reduces database query latency from 50-100ms to 1-5ms for cached content, improving user experience and reducing RDS CPU utilization by 60-80% for read-heavy workloads. The db.t3.medium RDS instance (2 vCPUs, 4 GB RAM vs. db.t3.micro 2 vCPUs, 1 GB RAM) handles increased connection pools and query complexity without throttling. CloudFront's 50% cache hit ratio offloads half the traffic from the origin, and the increased data transfer allocation (2,000 GB vs. 200 GB) supports 10x growth. Auto Scaling enables right-sizing: running 2 instances during normal hours and scaling to 6 only during the 60-hour monthly peak optimizes resource utilization.\n\n**Cost Optimization**: Total monthly cost is $549.01, within the $750 budget (73% utilization). ElastiCache ($99.28) eliminates the need for sticky sessions at the ALB and reduces database load, avoiding the need for a more expensive RDS instance class. The larger instance type (t3.medium) costs more per hour ($0.0416 vs. $0.0104) but provides better price-performance with fewer instances needed overall. RDS Multi-AZ ($99.28) costs double a single-AZ instance but eliminates the need for a separate read replica for high availability, reducing operational complexity. The architecture scales horizontally during the 60-hour peak (8% of the month) rather than over-provisioning for peak 24/7, saving approximately 66% on compute costs compared to running 6 instances continuously. Reserved Instances or Savings Plans could reduce costs by 30-40% for the baseline 2-instance deployment in a production commitment.\n\n**Sustainability**: The right-sized architecture minimizes resource waste by scaling compute capacity to match demand. Running 2 instances baseline and scaling to 6 only during peak hours (8% of the month) reduces energy consumption by 66% compared to continuously running peak capacity. The t3.medium instance type uses AWS Graviton-based processors where available, improving performance per watt. ElastiCache reduces database queries by 60-80%, lowering RDS CPU cycles and energy consumption. CloudFront's edge caching reduces data transfer from the origin, minimizing network energy costs. S3's storage efficiency and lifecycle policies can archive old photos to S3 Glacier for long-term sustainability.",
